        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Modern Drama",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                  MODERN DRAMA — LANDSCAPE                        |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                  |\n|  1870-1900: NATURALISM                                           |\n|  Ibsen: social critique, retrospective exposition, subtext      |\n|  Strindberg: psychological violence, dream play structure       |\n|  Chekhov: atmosphere, inaction, the non-event as drama         |\n|                                                                  |\n|  1900-1930: EXPRESSIONISM AND ALTERNATIVES                       |\n|  German expressionism: distorted reality, inner vision          |\n|  (Toller, Kaiser, Wedekind)                                     |\n|  Symbolism: Maeterlinck, Yeats — non-naturalistic               |\n|  Pirandello: identity, illusion, metatheatre                    |\n|                                                                  |\n|  1930-1950: POLITICAL THEATER AND BRECHT                         |\n|  Brecht's Verfremdungseffekt (V-Effekt)                          |\n|  Epic theater vs. dramatic theater                              |\n|  Living Newspaper (Federal Theater Project, USA)               |\n|  Documentary theater                                             |\n|                                                                  |\n|  1950-1970: THEATRE OF THE ABSURD                                |\n|  Beckett: Waiting for Godot, Endgame, Happy Days               |\n|  Ionesco: The Bald Soprano, Rhinoceros                          |\n|  Genet, Pinter, Adamov                                          |\n|                                                                  |\n|  CONTEMPORARY: postmodern and beyond                             |\n|  Robert Wilson: visual theater, slow time                       |\n|  Suzan-Lori Parks, Caryl Churchill: history/identity           |\n|  Immersive theater: Punchdrunk, Sleep No More                  |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```"
    },
