        "title": "Modern Drama",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Henrik Ibsen (1828–1906) is the origin of modern drama in the same sense that Darwin is the origin of modern biology: everything after him is shaped by the terms he set."
    },
    {
      "id": "04-MODERN-DRAMA.md#008",
      "path": [
        "04-MODERN-DRAMA.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:theater-performance:04-modern-drama, git-history:theater-performance:04-modern-drama]",
        "canonical_path": "theater-performance/04-MODERN-DRAMA.md",
        "concepts": "[modern, drama]",
        "current_path": "theater-performance/04-MODERN-DRAMA.md",
        "id": "maxim:theater-performance:modern-drama",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "theater-performance",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[modern, drama]",
        "section": "theater-performance",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Modern Drama",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nIBSEN'S FORMAL INNOVATIONS\n----------------------------\n1. THE WELL-MADE PLAY TURNED AGAINST ITSELF\n   The bien fait (Scribe, 1820s-50s) was a mechanical\n   formula: meticulous exposition, a letter or document\n   that creates crisis, a \"scène à faire\" (obligatory\n   scene of confrontation), a tidy denouement.\n   Ibsen uses this structure but fills it with real\n   psychological and social consequence. The \"tidy\"\n   conclusion is no longer available.\n   A Doll's House ends not with resolution but with\n   a door slam that opens onto the unknown."
    },
    {
      "id": "04-MODERN-DRAMA.md#009",
      "path": [
        "04-MODERN-DRAMA.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:theater-performance:04-modern-drama, git-history:theater-performance:04-modern-drama]",
        "canonical_path": "theater-performance/04-MODERN-DRAMA.md",
        "concepts": "[modern, drama]",
        "current_path": "theater-performance/04-MODERN-DRAMA.md",
        "id": "maxim:theater-performance:modern-drama",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "theater-performance",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[modern, drama]",
        "section": "theater-performance",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Modern Drama",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "2. RETROSPECTIVE STRUCTURE\n   Ibsen's plays are about the PAST catching up to the present.\n   The action is discovery: what happened before the\n   play began? The \"play\" is the revelation.\n   Rosmersholm, Ghosts, Hedda Gabler, The Master Builder —\n   all structured around the irruption of suppressed\n   past into a present that cannot contain it.\n   \"The past never stays past\" — this is Ibsen's thesis."
    },
    {
      "id": "04-MODERN-DRAMA.md#010",
      "path": [
        "04-MODERN-DRAMA.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:theater-performance:04-modern-drama, git-history:theater-performance:04-modern-drama]",
        "canonical_path": "theater-performance/04-MODERN-DRAMA.md",
        "concepts": "[modern, drama]",
        "current_path": "theater-performance/04-MODERN-DRAMA.md",
        "id": "maxim:theater-performance:modern-drama",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "theater-performance",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[modern, drama]",
        "section": "theater-performance",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Modern Drama",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "3. SUBTEXT: WHAT IS NOT SAID\n   Characters talk around what they mean.\n   Hedda Gabler never says she envies Lovborg's freedom.\n   She talks about \"vine leaves in his hair\" — an image,\n   not a statement. Ibsen invented the subtext that\n   Chekhov developed and Pinter made into a style."
    },
    {
      "id": "04-MODERN-DRAMA.md#011",
      "path": [
        "04-MODERN-DRAMA.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:theater-performance:04-modern-drama, git-history:theater-performance:04-modern-drama]",
        "canonical_path": "theater-performance/04-MODERN-DRAMA.md",
        "concepts": "[modern, drama]",
        "current_path": "theater-performance/04-MODERN-DRAMA.md",
        "id": "maxim:theater-performance:modern-drama",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "theater-performance",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[modern, drama]",
        "section": "theater-performance",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Modern Drama",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "4. THE PROBLEM PLAY\n   Drama as vehicle for social analysis:\n   A Doll's House (1879): the institution of bourgeois marriage\n   Ghosts (1881): hereditary syphilis and moral hypocrisy\n   An Enemy of the People (1882): expert knowledge vs. mob\n   The Wild Duck (1884): the destructive \"ideal demand\"\n```"
    },
